<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamzah Fansuri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hamzah Fansuri was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poet and scholar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> in the late 16th century. His mystical poems, written in the syair form, are among the earliest known works of Malay literature and made Malay a language of Sufi thought. His teaching on the unity of being was later condemned as heretical by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuruddin-ar-raniri">Nuruddin ar-Raniri</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Most of what is known of Hamzah Fansuri's life comes from the takhallus bait, the pen-name stanza that ends each of his poems, from the work of his disciple Hasan Fansuri and from commentaries on his poems, and many details are uncertain. His name suggests that he came from Barus, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> called Fansur, or spent much of his life there. A link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in Siam has also been proposed, though he may have travelled there rather than been born there.</p><p>He joined a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order and is thought to have worked at the court of the Aceh Sultanate. He travelled widely, visiting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, Mughal India, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and was one of the earliest Southeast Asians known to have made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His thought drew on medieval Islamic scholars, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi">Ibn Arabi</a>'s doctrine of Waḥdat al-Wujūd, the unity of being, then popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and Mughal India, which taught that all reality is one and that everything that exists is part of the divine. Hamzah saw God as immanent in all things, including the individual, and sought union with the indwelling spirit of God. He used the doctrine of seven stages of emanation, the martabat, through which God manifests himself in the world, ending in the Perfect Man.</p><p>Thirty-two of his poems survive, usually of 13 to 15 stanzas and some of up to 21, each naming him in its last stanza. Scholars have noted his technical skill in rhyme, his blending of Arabic words into Malay verse and his fondness for puns. Three prose works survive: Sharab al-'ashiqin, The Lovers' Beverage; Asrar al-'arifin, The Secrets of the Gnostics; and Kitab al-Muntahi, The Adept, a collection of Arabic and Persian quotations discussed in Malay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His death is generally placed around 1590 or earlier, though a later date in the reign of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a> has also been proposed. His teachings were promoted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> theologian Shamsuddin al-Sumatrani. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuruddin-ar-raniri">Nuruddin ar-Raniri</a> judged them heretical, holding that they did not conform to the belief that God remains unchanged by his creation, Sultana Taj ul-Alam, under his influence, tried to erase Hamzah's works and name, and his writings were burnt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hamzah Fansuri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hamzah Fansuri</b> wrote mystical poetry that made Malay a medium of Sufi thought; Syamsuddin of Pasai served Iskandar Muda as chief scholar; Nuruddin ar-Raniri, a Gujarati scholar at the court of Iskandar Thani, wrote the encyclopaedic Bustan al-Salatin and attacked the mystical teachings of his predecessors; and Abdurrauf…</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
